{
  "name": "Iris",
  "notes": "An aperture built out of blades, not out of noise. `tile` on a single `line` IS a set of blades -- one shape repeated N times across the strip -- and driving the tile `count` between two and nine opens and closes the leaf count while the `mirror` under it makes the whole assembly symmetric about a wandering centre. The `scale` on top zooms the finished aperture. Three warps, all moving, on one emitter that never moves at all: `centre` stays at 0.5 for the entire patch and everything you see is the space being deformed.\n\nThat is worth reading beside `08-liquify`, which warps a stream of objects. Here the object is stationary and the warp chain is the entire animation, which is the cheapest possible way to get complex motion -- one `line` costs one trapezoid integration per pixel however many blades the tile makes of it.\n\nThe breathing is a two-stage chain over a period of nearly three minutes: `M3` drives `M1.rate`, and `M1` drives the zoom, so the aperture's pulse is a rate that is itself rising and falling. `M3` also drives the blade width and the layer opacity together, so a slow breath is dimmer AND thinner-bladed than a fast one. Ultraviolet run with a single acid lime almost exactly opposite, and the lime sits at the end of the palette so only the blade edges catch it.",
